#!/usr/bin/python3
"""Choose the FlickOS desktop layout, style, wallpaper, desktop clicks and apps button.

A layout (panel arrangement and window buttons) is a folder
/usr/share/flickos/layouts/ID/ with a layout.ini. A style (Dark or Light: GTK
theme, icons, window theme and the colors of panel, terminal, launcher and
notifications) is a folder /usr/share/flickos/styles/ID/ with a style.ini.
Users make their own styles in ~/.local/share/flickos/styles/ID/ (`style
new`): the first folder with an id wins, and a style.ini with Inherits=ID
takes every key it lacks from that style and adds its palette files after
the parent's, so it holds only what differs. `check` says what is wrong
with a style, or with everything the user has; `style set` refuses a style
with errors. A theme a style names that isn't installed is replaced by its
parent's.

`explain` shows, for each program FlickOS sets up, the files it reads and
which one wins: what to look at when a change doesn't show. `watch`, run in
a terminal, applies each save to the user's own styles, add-ons and labwc
files at once (inotify, nothing runs while nothing is saved).

A user's style is shared as one file: `export ID` writes ID.tar.gz with what
the user's own styles set (the parent's colors folded in when the parent is
the user's too), `import FILE` takes one after checking every header, file
name and size, then `check` (only colors: nothing in a style can run a
program). `try ID` uses a style until the next login: a record of what it
changed in GSettings lets every login put that back, also after the tried
style was deleted.

labwc merges the user's own files (rc.xml, menu.xml, environment,
themerc-override) over FlickOS's (labwc -m: flickos-session passes it while
the labwc-merge marker exists, which `prepare` writes at login), unless
merging would break FlickOS (merge_decision): the live or plain session, a
user rc.xml that is a copy of FlickOS's or has <default />, a user menu.xml
with FlickOS's menu ids, or a user autostart. Then the user's files replace
FlickOS's, as before, and `doctor` says why and what to change.

The plain session (FLICKOS_PLAIN=1, flickos-session-plain) rules out the
user's own changes: users' styles (a user style in use becomes its FlickOS
ancestor), add-ons, ~/.config/waybar and ~/.config/mako are ignored, choices
can't be changed, and plain/labwc/ holds links to FlickOS's own labwc files
only, for labwc -C. It changes no file of the user's.

Both are applied through a runtime overlay in $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/flickos/ (RAM,
per login): the session script puts its xdg/ folder first in XDG_CONFIG_DIRS,
and files there are regenerated at every login and never written to $HOME, so
FlickOS updates keep reaching users. A file that isn't needed is deleted, and
the program falls back to the system file.

The panel and notifications always run from generated files in
$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/flickos/: waybar/ has the layout's bars with the pinned apps
filled in and a stylesheet that imports the style's palette and the layout's
CSS; mako/config includes the system config and the style's palette. A layout
with a dock (Dock=, an sfwbar config: Cupertino's dock, Traditional's window
list) also gets dock/sfwbar.config: its config with the pinned apps filled in,
then the style's palette and the layout's DockStyle= CSS. sfwbar runs next to
waybar and is replaced along with it. A layout with Desktops= gets that many
labwc desktops, and their keyboard shortcuts, in the overlay rc.xml.

The user's tweaks, ~/.config/flickos/tweaks/style.css (`tweaks new` writes a
starter), are imported last into the panel's stylesheet and copied after the
dock's CSS. waybar stops on any CSS error, so every CSS file of the user's
(tweaks, their add-ons' and their own layout's) is parsed first by GTK in a
child (/usr/libexec/flickos/check-css), and a file it can't read is left out
(a user layout's CSS replaced by FlickOS's base.css); `check` names the line.

Panel, dock and notifications run under a supervisor (`supervise`), which
restarts one that fails: autostart runs once, so nothing else would bring the
panel back. One stopped on purpose (`set`, or the end of the session) stays
stopped. What they print goes to $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/flickos/panel.log, together
with this program's own warnings while it runs the panel.

Choice: ~/.config/flickos/layout (style, clicks, launcher, menu-style, clock,
quick-settings), else [Defaults] Layout= (Style=, Clicks=, Launcher=,
MenuStyle=, Clock=, QuickSettings=) in
/etc/xdg/flickos/layouts.conf, else the built-in default. Unknown ids fall back.
Choosing "default" removes the user's choice. Every change that shows is
recorded in $XDG_STATE_HOME/flickos/history.jsonl, where Settings > History
can undo it (flickos-control history). Clicks (what a click on the empty
desktop does) are built in, not files, and are applied through the overlay
rc.xml. The clock (24- or 12-hour) is built in too: the generated panel's
clock formats get %I:%M %p for %H:%M. Its tooltip calendar marks today in the
style's selection colors.

The apps button (launcher) opens flickos-menu's start menu (the default while
flickos-menu is installed), or fuzzel if the user chose the app launcher.
While the start menu is on for a layout with a Menu= corner, the overlay
points the apps button, a tap of Super and desktop clicks at it, menu/anchor
holds the corner and the menu style (the layout's MenuStyle=, unless the user
chose one for all layouts: menu-style), and the panel runs its daemon;
turning it off stops the daemon.
While it is off, none of this exists.

Quick Settings (flickos-menu's second window) is off by default. While it is
on for a layout with a Quick= corner, menu/quick holds the corner, the
generated panel's volume and battery icons open it (grouped, so they light
as one), Super+A opens it and desktop clicks close it, and the panel runs
flickos-menu's daemon (which runs while the start menu, Quick Settings or
the phone popup is on).

The phone popup (flickos-menu's third window) follows the phone connection.
While that is on and flickos-menu is installed, for a layout with a Quick=
corner, menu/phone holds the corner and the panel's phone button opens the
popup. Otherwise the button keeps flickos-phone menu.

Other FlickOS programs add labwc config fragments, merged into the overlay
rc.xml in this order: flickos-control's mouse, touchpad and keyboard options
($XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/flickos/control/labwc.xml), then, while tiling is on,
flick-tiler's regions, window rule and keybinds (tiler/labwc.xml).
flickos-control's keyboard layout (control/environment) goes into an overlay
labwc environment file, after the system one: labwc reads only the first it
finds. `reconfigure` regenerates the overlay and reloads labwc.

Add-ons are programs that aren't FlickOS packages (yet, like experiments)
but want a place on the desktop: a folder ~/.config/flickos/addons/NAME/ (or
/usr/share/flickos/addons/NAME/; the user's wins) with any of labwc.xml
(keybinds and window rules, merged after the fragments above, never taking
a key the desktop binds; a keybind's description="…" goes to the shortcut
sheet, not labwc), waybar.jsonc (custom/ modules the layout doesn't have,
placed next to the phone's button) and style.css (imported after the
layout's, named colors only). `addons` lists them; `reconfigure`
and `reload-panel` apply a change. Without any, nothing changes.

The wallpaper is the layout's (Wallpaper=), else the style's, shown with
swaybg, unless the user chose their own: in waypaper (the Wallpaper app), or
~/.config/flickos/wallpaper. Switching the layout or the style switches only a
FlickOS wallpaper.

GTK settings are changed only by `style set`, and gtk-theme and icon-theme only
while they hold one of the styles' themes, so a theme chosen in nwg-look stays.
`set` gives windows that draw their own title bar (Firefox, GTK header bars)
the layout's window buttons (button-layout), only while that setting holds its
default or one of the layouts' values.

`pick` is the chooser: a GTK 3 window (python3-gi, so it follows the Arc theme),
or fuzzel --dmenu when GTK can't be used. It is the only part that needs more
than the standard library. `first-run` opens it once at the first login of a new
account on an installed system.

Exit codes: 0 ok, 1 error (doctor: warnings found), 2 usage error.
"""

import argparse
